@page "/render-modes"
@using ZenithUI.Demo.Client.Components

@*
    The page itself has no @rendermode, so it is static SSR. Each card below opts into its own
    mode. The same component type renders three ways on one page - which is the whole claim the
    library makes about render-mode agnosticism, made observable.
*@

<PageTitle>Render modes &middot; ZenithUI</PageTitle>

<header class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Render modes</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-content-muted">
        ZenithUI declares <code>@@rendermode</code> nowhere. The host application decides, per
        component, and the library has to cope with every answer &mdash; including "none".
    </p>
</header>

<section class="mt-10 grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-3">
    <RenderModeProbe Title="Static SSR"
                     Description="No render mode. HTML only - the counter does nothing, and its toggle icon is stuck on the prerender default. Both are correct." />

    <RenderModeProbe Title="Interactive Server"
                     Description="Runs over a SignalR circuit. JS interop goes through the connection."
                     @rendermode="InteractiveServer" />

    <RenderModeProbe Title="Interactive WebAssembly"
                     Description="Runs in the browser runtime, with its own DI container and IJSRuntime."
                     @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
</section>

<section class="mt-10 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
    <article class="rounded-zen-lg border border-border bg-surface-raised p-6 shadow-zen-sm">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">What the static card proves</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-content-muted">
            Its counter does not increment and its theme toggle does not respond &mdash; there is no
            .NET running in the browser for that markup. What matters is that it still
            <em>renders</em>: correct layout, correct palette, correct ARIA attributes, no
            exception from a JS interop call that was never attempted.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-content-muted">
            <code>ZenJsComponentBase</code> is what guarantees that. It checks
            <code>RendererInfo.IsInteractive</code> before importing a module, so a prerender pass
            never reaches for a JavaScript runtime that is not attached yet.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-content-muted">
            Its toggle icon is the one honest wart: <code>localStorage</code> is not sent with the
            request, so the server cannot know the stored preference and renders the default. The
            other two cards correct themselves once their runtime attaches; this one has nothing to
            correct itself with. The page around it is still the right colour, because the inline
            <code>ZenThemeScript</code> settled that before first paint.
        </p>
    </article>

    <article class="rounded-zen-lg border border-border bg-surface-raised p-6 shadow-zen-sm">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Why the theme still works everywhere</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-content-muted">
            Change the theme from the header and all three cards repaint together, including the
            static one. Nothing re-rendered it: the palette lives in <code>--zen-*</code> custom
            properties on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, so the browser restyles the whole document
            without Blazor being involved at all.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-content-muted">
            This is the practical argument for semantic tokens over <code>dark:</code> variants. A
            <code>dark:</code>-based component would need a class change to switch palettes, and a
            statically rendered component has no way to make one.
        </p>
    </article>
</section>
